{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1789384440,"nonce":"r2P7Jyk4kV7r5FH4jRHpWcbuVCA=","title":"Dead stores from a constructor's defaults survive inlining","message":"`Class$new` fills every field with its default, then `__init__` stores\nthe real value. Inlined into a caller, the block holds two stores to\neach field of one fresh allocation with nothing reading it between;\n`bench_op_overload.py` did four dead stores per iteration.\n\n`dead_store` removes them: per block, for pointers derived from a\n`malloc` in that block by constant-offset geps and casts, a store whose\nexact bytes are stored again before any load of them is dropped. An\nallocation whose address escapes (stored, passed to a call, cast to an\ninteger, indexed by a variable) stops being tracked from that point.\nRuns in the fixed point after `scalar_replace_alloc`.\n\nop_overload run 67 to 63.6 ms; op_overload_ref 8.9 to 7.1 (ZynML 6.1).","files":null}]}